        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">José de San Martín</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1778 CE – 1850 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">25 February 1778 (the year is disputed; most documents point to 1777 or 1778) in Yapeyú, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrientes">Corrientes</a>, an Indian reduction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarani-people">Guaraní people</a>; son of the Spanish officer Juan de San Martín and Gregoria Matorras del Ser</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">17 August 1850 at three o'clock, in his house at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne-sur-Mer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, almost blind and in poor health, shortly after news of the Argentine victory against the Anglo-French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Spanish army officer; colonel and commander of the Regiment of Mounted Grenadiers; commander of the Army of the North (1814); governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a> from 6 September 1814; leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a>; Protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from 1821 to 1822</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the Regiment of Mounted Grenadiers and the battle of San Lorenzo; organising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a>; the Crossing of the Andes; the victories of Chacabuco and Maipú in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>; the naval expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and the declaration of its independence in 1821; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> meeting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a></span></div>
