    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born in a Guaraní mission town and educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, San Martín served the Spanish crown for more than twenty years, fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> and then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> to serve the independence movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in 1812.</li><li>As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a> he raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a>, led it across the mountains in about a month and won at Chacabuco and Maipú, the battle that secured Chilean independence, before sailing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and declaring its independence on 28 July 1821.</li><li>After a closed-door meeting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> in July 1822, whose content historians can only infer, he resigned as Protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, left politics and the army, and spent the rest of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Crossing the Andes was not the first military expedition to cross the range; what set San Martín's apart was the size of his army and that it had to be ready for combat right after the crossing.</p></aside>
    </section>

