    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1778</span><span class="rf-text">San Martín is born in Yapeyú, in the Guaraní missions; his father was the local governor</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1812</span><span class="rf-text">He reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> on 9 March and begins to raise the Regiment of Mounted Grenadiers</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1814</span><span class="rf-text">He takes office as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a> on 6 September and begins organising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1817</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> wins at Chacabuco on 12 February and enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> the next day</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1818</span><span class="rf-text">The patriots defeat the royalist army at Maipú, which secures Chilean independence</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1821</span><span class="rf-text">After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, he declares the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> on 28 July and becomes Protector</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1822</span><span class="rf-text">He meets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> on 26 July and soon resigns as Protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1850</span><span class="rf-text">He dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne-sur-Mer</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>José Francisco de San Martín y Matorras was born on 25 February 1778 in Yapeyú, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrientes">Corrientes</a>, an Indian reduction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarani-people">Guaraní people</a>. The year is disputed: no baptism record exists, and later documents such as passports, military records and wedding papers gave him varying ages, most pointing to 1777 or 1778. His father, Juan de San Martín, born in Cervatos de la Cueza in the present Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palencia">Palencia</a>, was a military man in the service of the Spanish Crown and in 1774 became Governor of the Yapeyú <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a>, part of the Government of the Guaraní Missions, which administered the thirty Guaraní <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions. The family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in 1781, and in 1783 Juan, who had asked for a transfer, left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>The family settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and then moved to Málaga, as Juan could not earn a promotion. San Martín began his studies at Málaga's school of temporalities in 1785 but probably did not finish the six years of elementary education before he enrolled in the Regiment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> in 1789, at the required age of 11, as a cadet. He fought against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melilla">Melilla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> in 1791 and was made sub-lieutenant in 1793. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-second-coalition">War of the Second Coalition</a>, when Spain was allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, his ship, the Santa Dorotea, was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, which held him as a prisoner of war for some time. He later fought around Cádiz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> as second captain of light infantry, took part in the War of the Oranges against Portugal in 1801 and was promoted captain in 1804. There the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> influenced him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda.jpg" alt="Francisco de Miranda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_de_Miranda_by_Tovar_y_Tovar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Tovar y Tovar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> in 1808 San Martín was named adjutant of Francisco Solano, 2nd Marquis de Socorro, then led a battalion of volunteers in the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>. For his part in the Spanish victory at Bailén on 19 July 1808 he received a gold medal and the rank of lieutenant colonel. He then resigned from the Spanish army for controversial reasons. Historians suggest that he missed his native land, that he was in British employ, or that both wars came from the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideas and absolutism. In 1811 he was initiated in the Lodge of Rational Knights, whose American-born members agreed to return home and join the revolutionary movements. He went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, met other South Americans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> house, and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> on 9 March 1812 to serve under the First Triumvirate.</p><p>The Triumvirate made him a lieutenant colonel of cavalry and asked him to create a cavalry unit, which became the Regiment of Mounted Grenadiers. In September 1812 he married María de los Remedios de Escalada, aged 14. With Carlos María de Alvear he intervened with military force in the Revolution of 8 October 1812, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> promoted him to colonel. At San Lorenzo his regiment charged royalists who had landed to pillage; his horse was killed and he was wounded in the face and arm. In 1814 he replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-belgrano">Manuel Belgrano</a> at the head of the Army of the North. After falling ill that April he devised a plan: raise an army in Mendoza, cross the Andes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and go on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> by sea. He became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a> on 6 September.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins.jpg" alt="Bernardo O'Higgins" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">Bernardo O'Higgins</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ohiggins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Gil de Castro, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a> San Martín organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a>. He drafted every citizen able to bear arms and all slaves aged 16 to 30, later extending the emancipation of slaves to those aged 14 to 55 and allowing them promotion to higher ranks, and a mix of incentives, confiscations and planned economy provisioned the army. He urged a declaration of independence before the crossing, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-tucuman">Congress of Tucumán</a> made one on 9 July 1816. At the end of 1816 the army had 5,000 men. It crossed the Andes in six columns over nearly a month, and only 4,300 mules and 511 horses survived, less than half the original number. At Chacabuco on 12 February 1817 San Martín, with 3,600 men against Rafael Maroto's 2,450, won by a pincer movement: 600 royalists died and 500 were taken prisoner, against 12 dead and 120 wounded in the Army of the Andes. He declined to be Supreme Director of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and proposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">Bernardo O'Higgins</a>.</p><p>After a night surprise at Cancha Rayada, where 120 patriots died and 1,000 fled, the reorganised army met the royalists at Maipú in 1818. There 500 royalists died in the last stand at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">hacienda</a> Lo Espejo and all top royalist leaders but Osorio were killed or captured, and the battle secured Chilean independence. A fleet led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cochrane-10th-earl-of-dundonald">Thomas Cochrane</a> then carried 4,000 soldiers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, sailing on 20 August 1820. San Martín declared Peruvian independence on 28 July 1821 and became Protector. His government abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a> and yanaconazgo, gave the natives citizenship, abolished the Inquisition and corporal punishment, and enacted freedom of speech.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-carrera"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-carrera.jpg" alt="José Miguel Carrera" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-carrera">José Miguel Carrera</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GeneralCarrera.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Miguel Venegas Cifuentes (1907-1979), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chilean Carrera brothers, who wished to act as a government in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a>, ignored local laws and their soldiers committed acts of vandalism; San Martín imprisoned them and sent them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, which began a rivalry. After Chacabuco <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-carrera">José Miguel Carrera</a> refused to place his fleet under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> and was imprisoned by Pueyrredón. His brothers Luis and Juan José were executed a few days before San Martín reached Mendoza; who took the initiative is controversial. The Chilean historian Benjamín Vicuña Mackenna indicts San Martín, while J. C. Raffo de la Reta blames O'Higgins. Manuel Rodríguez was killed in prison, perhaps on the decision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lautaro">Lautaro</a> lodge, and San Martín was by then on the way to Buenos Aires.</p><p>Support from Buenos Aires was uncertain. Pueyrredón thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> should repay the money spent on its liberation and later delivered only a third of the funds promised for the navy. When the army was ordered back to Buenos Aires during the civil war, San Martín resigned and its officers refused to go, and the unitarians long resented his refusal to aid the Supreme Directors. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> Cochrane disputed his actions, took the navy away and later accused him in Chile. San Martín did not abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Peru, which had 40,000 slaveowners, but declared a freedom of wombs that freed the sons of slaves, and slaves joined his army in smaller numbers than expected.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-de-monteagudo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernardo-de-monteagudo.jpg" alt="Bernardo de Monteagudo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-de-monteagudo">Bernardo de Monteagudo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernardo_de_Monteagudo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">V.S. Noroña, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>, Bolívar annexed the city to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, and the two liberators met privately on 26 and 27 July 1822. There were no witnesses or minutes, so the content of their talks can only be inferred from later actions and letters; likely topics were reinforcements and a combined army with San Martín second to Bolívar. They differed on government: Bolívar proposed a republic, San Martín a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>. While San Martín was away, the minister Bernardo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-de-monteagudo">Monteagudo</a> was removed by a rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. San Martín resigned as Protector a few days later and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a>. Historians give several reasons: the compromised discipline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a>, the rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and Colombia, the lack of support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, O'Higgins and Cochrane, and his refusal to rule as a dictator.</p><p>His wife died in 1823 and he took their daughter Mercedes Tomasa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. In 1837 he offered his services to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> against the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>; Rosas declined because of San Martín's age. He bequeathed his curved saber to Rosas. After the French revolution of 1848 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne-sur-Mer</a>, almost blind and in poor health. He died there at three o'clock on 17 August 1850, shortly after news of the Argentine victory against the Anglo-French blockade.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-avellaneda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicolas-avellaneda.jpg" alt="Nicolás Avellaneda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-avellaneda">Nicolás Avellaneda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicol%C3%A1s_Avellaneda_-_1910.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>San Martín asked in his will to be taken to the cemetery without any funeral and to be moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> afterwards. He lay in the crypt of the Basilica of Notre-Dame de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> from 1850 to 1861, and the unrest after Rosas's fall delayed the transfer. His remains were repatriated on 29 May 1880, under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-avellaneda">Nicolás Avellaneda</a>, and his mausoleum stands in the Buenos Aires Metropolitan Cathedral, in a wing expanded because he was suspected of being a freemason.</p><p>The Federals acclaimed him as a national hero of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> in his lifetime and after his death, while the unitarians resented his refusal to aid the Supreme Directors and his support for Rosas. The unitarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-mitre">Bartolomé Mitre</a> wrote a biography that made San Martín universally acclaimed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">the Liberator</a> of Argentina, but it introduced inaccuracies that served Mitre's political project and that later historians corrected. San Martín is regarded as a national hero of Argentina, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and the Order of the Liberator General San Martín is the highest decoration the Argentine government confers. Statues stand in most Argentine cities and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, and an equestrian statue was inaugurated in Boulogne-sur-Mer on 24 October 1909.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>José de San Martín (1778–1850), an officer of the Spanish army born in the Guaraní missions, organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a>, won at Chacabuco and Maipú and sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, whose independence he declared in 1821. After meeting Bolívar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> he resigned and lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> until his death.</em></p>

